1
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:05,840
I'm thinking about growing a goatee.

2
00:00:05,840 --> 00:00:07,370
Oh, actually that's a Van Dyke.

3
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:09,210
A goatee is just hair on the chin.

4
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:12,280
Oh. Wait, then what is it if you just have hair up here?

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:13,280
You mean a moustache?

6
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:15,400
- Moo-stache! - He said it!

7
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,820
Ha-ha, very funny. Make fun of the foreign guy.

8
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,790
For your information, there are four times as many

9
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:22,520
Indians as there are Americans, so the way we say it is right.

10
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:23,790
- Say what? - Moo-stache.

11
00:00:23,790 --> 00:00:25,340
- Moo-stache! - He said "Moo-stache"

12
00:00:26,530 --> 00:00:28,430
Guys, you're being childish.

13
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:30,230
Yeah, she's right. You're grown men,

14
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:31,730
the kind who are perfectly capable

15
00:00:31,730 --> 00:00:33,690
of growing your own moo-staches.

16
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,240
Hey, you were funny on purpose, good job.

17
00:00:39,070 --> 00:00:41,510
Hi. Sorry I'm late-- I was at an audition.

18
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:44,340
- Oh, I'm sorry. - You'll get 'em next time.

19
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:47,510
How about instead of assuming I failed,

20
00:00:47,510 --> 00:00:50,000
- you ask me how it went? - Sorry.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,930
- How did it go? - Just shut up.

22
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:54,250
Here, you can have your seat.

23
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:56,440
No, no, no, stay there. I'm fine on the floor.

24
00:00:56,710 --> 00:00:59,270
Have you guys ever thought about getting a dining room table?

25
00:00:59,270 --> 00:01:01,770
Yeah. You actually do have room for one up there.

26
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:04,630
Oh, sure, I sit on the floor for years, no one cares.

27
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:06,110
The pretty white girl's there ten seconds,

28
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:08,770
and suddenly we're all running to IKEA.

29
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:11,740
No one is running anywhere.

30
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:13,700
We're not getting a dining room table.

31
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,110
I know you don't like change,

32
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:16,690
but it's not a terrible idea.

33
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:18,580
Yeah, you guys never use that space up there.

34
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:19,370
Why not get a table?

35
00:01:19,370 --> 00:01:21,680
Do you want the long answer or the short answer?

36
00:01:21,680 --> 00:01:24,200
Hey, how come we never get that option?

37
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:28,920
Chaos theory suggests that even in a deterministic system,

38
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:31,990
if the equations describing its behavior are nonlinear,

39
00:01:31,990 --> 00:01:34,240
a tiny change in the initial conditions

40
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:37,720
can lead to a cataclysmic and unpredictable result.

41
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:39,020
Translation?

42
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:42,270
Waah. I don't want a table!

43
00:02:10,770 --> 00:02:13,670
Hey, Penny and I are gonna go shop for a dining room table.

44
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:14,830
You want to come with us?

45
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,410
You know, I'd love to,

46
00:02:16,410 --> 00:02:18,880
but, um, I'm too busy

47
00:02:18,880 --> 00:02:22,480
falling back in love with Windows 98.

48
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,180
Seriously? You haven't used this desk in years.

49
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,270
The second I want to get rid of it, you're up here working?

50
00:02:28,270 --> 00:02:29,750
I can't talk right now--

51
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:33,260
I have several thousand updates to install.

52
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:35,300
Are you really gonna sit here all day?

53
00:02:35,610 --> 00:02:40,000
Think of me as Arthur Dent in Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy,

54
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:43,370
lying in front of the bulldozers to protect his home.

55
00:02:43,370 --> 00:02:46,130
If you'll recall, the Vogon fleet blew up the Earth anyway.

56
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:47,450
It's a made-up story, Leonard--

57
00:02:47,450 --> 00:02:49,550
I don't even know why you're talking about it.

58
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,030
I'm putting all this stuff in storage.

59
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:53,090
We don't need any of it.

60
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:55,140
You... How can you say that?

61
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:58,450
You show me one thing in here we can live without.

62
00:02:58,450 --> 00:02:59,350
Oh, hang on.

63
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,260
Check your in-box.

64
00:03:07,030 --> 00:03:09,840
Wait. Is this really worth it?

65
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:12,100
We've lived together for years

66
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,500
with nary an argument. Huh?

67
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:17,100
But we start talking about a table,

68
00:03:17,100 --> 00:03:19,810
and suddenly we're at each other's throats.

69
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:23,400
"Nary an argument"?

70
00:03:24,190 --> 00:03:26,170
"Nary"?!

71
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:30,310
Well, that means "not one," or "not any""

72
00:03:30,690 --> 00:03:33,650
Maybe instead of a table, you should buy a dictionary.

73
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:37,710
Well, I don't know if I won that,

74
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:39,730
but at least he's upset.

75
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:42,770
Check it out.

76
00:03:45,560 --> 00:03:47,530
Magic wand TV remote!

77
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:50,150
Yeah, I can control all sorts of stuff.

78
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:51,830
Did Bernadette think it was cool?

79
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:54,780
Not when I said "mute" and pointed it at her face.

80
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:56,940
Oh, uh, let me try.

81
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:01,100
Channelis changeroni!

82
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:05,730
This might be my second favorite brown magic wand.

83
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:13,130
Well, that's the last time I play with that.

84
00:04:16,930 --> 00:04:18,670
I have to take this.

85
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:20,130
Hello?

86
00:04:21,520 --> 00:04:22,920
Off.

87
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,730
And on.

88
00:04:24,730 --> 00:04:26,970
And the Oprah Network.

89
00:04:27,800 --> 00:04:31,200
This is so much better than watching TV like a muggle.

90
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:33,670
Okay. Thank you.

91
00:04:33,670 --> 00:04:35,650
We'll be in touch.

92
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:39,760
You're not gonna believe this.

93
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:41,640
What's going on?

94
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:44,100
Oh. Wait.

95
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:45,640
On!

96
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:47,850
And off!

97
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:50,150
What's going on?

98
00:04:53,510 --> 00:04:55,380
That was NASA.

99
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:59,690
They want me to go back up to the space station.

100
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:01,690
Wow. What did you tell them?

101
00:05:01,690 --> 00:05:04,000
I told them I'd be honored.

102
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,970
A second trip to space-- I'll miss you.

103
00:05:06,970 --> 00:05:08,430
And I'm gonna miss you, too.

104
00:05:08,430 --> 00:05:09,670
I just want you to know, I'm happy

105
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:12,260
- to look in on Bernadette while you're gone. - Thank you.

106
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:13,840
And if anything were to happen to you,

107
00:05:13,840 --> 00:05:16,670
we will name our firstborn son Howard.

108
00:05:18,340 --> 00:05:19,590
I'm just kidding.

109
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:22,050
We'll name him Dalib, after my grandfather.

110
00:05:24,770 --> 00:05:26,540
Ooh, this one looks nice.

111
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:29,890
No. Sheldon doesn't like reclaimed wood.

112
00:05:30,590 --> 00:05:31,690
Why not?

113
00:05:31,690 --> 00:05:35,140
He's afraid the original owners will come back.

114
00:05:35,570 --> 00:05:37,620
Yeah. Well, Sheldon's not here.

115
00:05:37,620 --> 00:05:39,510
Well, he is here.

116
00:05:40,090 --> 00:05:41,510
So unless you want to dig him out

117
00:05:41,510 --> 00:05:43,080
with a bone saw and a melon baller,

118
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:44,990
there's nothing I can do about it.

119
00:05:45,020 --> 00:05:46,950
All right, sweetie, you're paying for this table,

120
00:05:46,950 --> 00:05:48,280
and it's your apartment, too.

121
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:49,900
- I know, but... - No buts.

122
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:52,620
You got to stop letting him boss you around.

123
00:05:52,620 --> 00:05:56,160
You're right. I mean, h-he decides what TV shows we watch,

124
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:58,560
what food we eat, who my favorite hobbit is--

125
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,930
I wanted Frodo, but we can't both have Frodo,

126
00:06:00,930 --> 00:06:04,700
so guess who's stuck with Samwise Gamgee.

127
00:06:04,890 --> 00:06:06,700
See? Right there. You're a grown man,

128
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:09,270
you should be able to pick whichever hobbit you want.

129
00:06:10,330 --> 00:06:13,660
Wish you wouldn't, but one problem at a time.

130
00:06:15,010 --> 00:06:17,720
Y-You make a lot of sense.

131
00:06:17,720 --> 00:06:20,350
I like this table, and I'm getting it.

132
00:06:20,350 --> 00:06:22,550
Really? This one?

133
00:06:24,370 --> 00:06:25,660
That one?

134
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:28,500
Damn right I like that one.

135
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:33,760
 Good news.

136
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:37,620
Someone in this room gets to take a ride on a rocket.

137
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:40,720
Fine. Can I at least shower first?

138
00:06:41,600 --> 00:06:42,650
No, not that.

139
00:06:42,650 --> 00:06:45,170
Although you already agreed to it, so no take-backs.

140
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:46,840
What are you talking about?

141
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:48,780
Sit down.

142
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:50,950
NASA called.

143
00:06:50,950 --> 00:06:52,910
The telescope mount I installed

144
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:55,500
on the space station got damaged,

145
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:58,980
and they want me to go back up and fix it.

146
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:03,000
Well, what did you say?

147
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:05,470
What do you think I...? I said yes!

148
00:07:05,470 --> 00:07:06,920
Why do you look surprised?

149
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:08,620
Well, it's just, after last time,

150
00:07:08,620 --> 00:07:10,700
I didn't think you'd ever want to go back.

151
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:11,550
Are you kidding?

152
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:14,830
- It was the greatest experience of my life. - Really?

153
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:16,840
'Cause I kind of remember a lot of complaining

154
00:07:16,840 --> 00:07:19,560
and wishing for it to be over.

155
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:21,460
I think you have me confused with

156
00:07:21,460 --> 00:07:24,520
what's gonna happen when you get out of that shower.

157
00:07:29,270 --> 00:07:31,000
Hey, all set. What do you think?

158
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:32,570
There's plenty of room for everybody,

159
00:07:32,570 --> 00:07:34,060
a view out the window.

160
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:36,720
My spot on the couch has a great view of a window.

161
00:07:36,720 --> 00:07:39,360
Sometimes I can see space battles through it.

162
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:41,340
It's called a TV.

163
00:07:42,130 --> 00:07:45,150
Give it a chance, Sheldon; you might actually like it.

164
00:07:45,940 --> 00:07:48,170
You're absolutely right.

165
00:07:50,300 --> 00:07:51,770
Nope.

166
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:56,470
Well, you can't say he didn't give it a fair shot.

167
00:07:56,470 --> 00:07:58,280
So, when can we get rid of it?

168
00:07:58,280 --> 00:07:59,670
We're not.

169
00:07:59,670 --> 00:08:01,480
What about the roommate agreement?

170
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:02,870
It specifically states

171
00:08:02,870 --> 00:08:04,470
that any changes in furnishing

172
00:08:04,470 --> 00:08:06,530
have to be approved by the Furnishing Committee.

173
00:08:06,530 --> 00:08:08,880
Which only sits on alternate years.

174
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:10,040
Yeah, and by the way,

175
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:12,220
it sits over there.

176
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,650
- Come on, that is ridiculous. - She's right--

177
00:08:14,650 --> 00:08:17,880
a committee that important should meet more often.

178
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:19,700
That's not what I'm saying.

179
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,150
This is the thing about me standing up to him

180
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:24,590
- and not letting him run my life? - Yes.

181
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:26,590
That.

182
00:08:27,050 --> 00:08:30,170
Okay. I think we've found the problem here.

183
00:08:30,170 --> 00:08:32,150
It's not the table at all.

184
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:34,310
- It's you. - Me?

185
00:08:34,310 --> 00:08:37,120
Well, it's always me-- take one for the team.

186
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:39,860
I have spent years turning this lump of clay

187
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:42,180
into an acceptable conduit for my will,

188
00:08:42,180 --> 00:08:44,640
and then you came along and reshaped him,

189
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:48,850
with your newfangled ideas and your fancy genitals.

190
00:08:49,390 --> 00:08:51,480
Are you gonna let him talk to me like this?

191
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:54,250
"Fancy" sounds like a compliment.

192
00:08:54,250 --> 00:08:56,390
Okay, I have not tried to change Leonard.

193
00:08:56,390 --> 00:08:57,970
That's just what happens in relationships.

194
00:08:57,970 --> 00:08:59,990
Look how much Amy's changed you.

195
00:08:59,990 --> 00:09:01,990
- That's not true. - Oh, please.

196
00:09:01,990 --> 00:09:03,800
When I first met you, you were incapable

197
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:05,290
of touching another human being.

198
00:09:05,290 --> 00:09:07,980
Now you're holding hands, you're going on dates,

199
00:09:07,980 --> 00:09:10,290
you even made out with her on a train.

200
00:09:10,290 --> 00:09:11,800
She told you?!

201
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:13,740
Of course she told me-- it's the most interesting thing

202
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,430
that's ever happened to her in her entire life!

203
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,260
You're too close to it,

204
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:21,610
but Amy has had a huge impact on you.

205
00:09:22,030 --> 00:09:24,000
You're right.

206
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,430
Without realizing it, I've allowed that woman

207
00:09:26,430 --> 00:09:28,560
to alter my personality.

208
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,370
Mm, Sheldon, you didn't have a personality;

209
00:09:30,370 --> 00:09:32,990
you just had some shows you liked.

210
00:09:35,270 --> 00:09:36,230
No.

211
00:09:36,230 --> 00:09:38,050
No, I've changed.

212
00:09:38,050 --> 00:09:40,520
Like the frog who's put in a pot of water

213
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:41,810
that's heated so gradually

214
00:09:41,810 --> 00:09:44,160
he doesn't realize he's boiling to death.

215
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:46,510
Or you're the frog who's been kissed by a princess

216
00:09:46,510 --> 00:09:47,920
and turned into a prince.

217
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,400
Or you're just a tall, annoying frog.

218
00:09:50,880 --> 00:09:52,500
Excuse me.

219
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:54,510
I have to break up with my girlfriend.

220
00:09:54,510 --> 00:09:56,940
- Oh, Sheldon, wait. - No.

221
00:09:56,940 --> 00:09:59,210
You've opened my eyes to the truth.

222
00:09:59,210 --> 00:10:01,510
Amy has made me a more affectionate,

223
00:10:01,510 --> 00:10:03,910
open-minded person.

224
00:10:03,910 --> 00:10:06,250
And that stops now.

225
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:11,440
- Well, we should call her. - Yeah, mm-hmm.

226
00:10:13,280 --> 00:10:14,470
Amy, it's Penny. Hey.

227
00:10:14,470 --> 00:10:15,870
Just a little heads-up.

228
00:10:15,870 --> 00:10:18,560
Leonard bought a dining room table.

229
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:21,110
Yeah. Sheldon's breaking up with you.

230
00:10:25,770 --> 00:10:27,790
Amy? Amy?

231
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:30,060
Amy?

232
00:10:30,570 --> 00:10:32,140
I'll get right to the point.

233
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:34,770
I think we need to end this relationship-- so...

234
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:38,260
just sign this with your finger, and, uh,

235
00:10:38,260 --> 00:10:40,530
please don't cry on my iPad--

236
00:10:40,530 --> 00:10:42,800
I didn't get AppleCare.

237
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:45,950
I'm not surprised you want to end the relationship.

238
00:10:45,950 --> 00:10:48,950
I'm a little surprised you didn't get AppleCare.

239
00:10:49,930 --> 00:10:52,410
Anyway, enjoy your life. Where do I sign?

240
00:10:52,410 --> 00:10:54,230
At the bottom. I must say,

241
00:10:54,230 --> 00:10:56,900
I'm relieved you're not making more of a scene out of this.

242
00:10:56,900 --> 00:10:58,600
Oh, I've already moved on.

243
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:00,810
Besides, this breakup has nothing to do with me.

244
00:11:01,010 --> 00:11:03,730
- Wh... It doesn't? - Of course not.

245
00:11:03,730 --> 00:11:05,970
This is just Leonard trying to take the focus off

246
00:11:05,970 --> 00:11:09,150
that dreadful table by sowing discord in our relationship.

247
00:11:09,150 --> 00:11:12,300
He's manipulating you like he always does.

248
00:11:12,790 --> 00:11:15,270
Wait-wait, now, hang on.

249
00:11:15,270 --> 00:11:17,210
You think he manipulates me?

250
00:11:17,210 --> 00:11:18,950
All the time.

251
00:11:19,300 --> 00:11:20,230
And he knew that,

252
00:11:20,230 --> 00:11:22,570
as your girlfriend, I wasn't gonna stand by

253
00:11:22,570 --> 00:11:24,980
and let him bring a table into your apartment.

254
00:11:24,980 --> 00:11:27,700
I mean, a table? Come on!

255
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:32,120
It is hideous.

256
00:11:32,120 --> 00:11:33,510
Well, thankfully, I won't have to see it,

257
00:11:33,510 --> 00:11:35,800
'cause I won't be your girlfriend anymore.

258
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:39,120
Amy Farrah Fowler.

259
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:41,450
Why, yes, I would like to take a survey.

260
00:11:41,980 --> 00:11:43,590
Wait.

261
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:45,910
You were really gonna stand by me

262
00:11:45,910 --> 00:11:48,180
against the dining room table?

263
00:11:48,600 --> 00:11:50,920
Of course I was.

264
00:11:50,930 --> 00:11:52,560
Yeah, wait.

265
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:56,430
How do I know that you're not manipulating me right now?

266
00:11:57,510 --> 00:11:59,010
I think if I were manipulating you,

267
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:01,640
you'd be smart enough to see it.

268
00:12:02,530 --> 00:12:04,180
How do I know you're not saying that

269
00:12:04,180 --> 00:12:06,980
as part of the manipulation?

270
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:10,070
I think you'd be smart enough to see that, too.

271
00:12:12,380 --> 00:12:13,720
Okay.

272
00:12:14,590 --> 00:12:17,400
I'm sorry I gave you such a hard time-- I just had to be sure.

273
00:12:20,790 --> 00:12:22,300
It's pretty exciting that NASA

274
00:12:22,300 --> 00:12:24,200
wants to send Howard back up.

275
00:12:24,200 --> 00:12:26,490
Yeah, exciting.

276
00:12:26,490 --> 00:12:28,810
- Can I ask you something? - Course.

277
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:31,760
He hated that entire experience.

278
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:33,140
Does it make me an awful wife

279
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:34,970
if I don't think he should do it again?

280
00:12:34,970 --> 00:12:36,360
Not at all.

281
00:12:36,450 --> 00:12:38,390
He's forgotten how miserable he was

282
00:12:38,390 --> 00:12:40,180
the entire time he was up there.

283
00:12:40,180 --> 00:12:43,200
It's like me in those moments when I miss India.

284
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:46,570
- So you'll talk to him? - Why me?

285
00:12:46,570 --> 00:12:48,890
Well, I'm his wife; I don't want to ruin it for him.

286
00:12:48,890 --> 00:12:49,970
That's the dynamic--

287
00:12:49,970 --> 00:12:52,050
I'm the fun one, you're the buzzkill.

288
00:12:52,050 --> 00:12:54,250
Since when am I the buzzkill?

289
00:12:54,690 --> 00:12:55,910
Do you think this is cool?

290
00:12:55,910 --> 00:12:57,160
No, I think it's stupid.

291
00:13:00,300 --> 00:13:02,200
You're right, it's great.

292
00:13:06,470 --> 00:13:07,310
Hello.

293
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:09,480
You guys didn't break up.

294
00:13:09,480 --> 00:13:11,550
No. Sorry to disappoint you,

295
00:13:11,550 --> 00:13:12,960
but Amy pointed out that

296
00:13:12,960 --> 00:13:15,020
you were only trying to manipulate me.

297
00:13:15,020 --> 00:13:16,440
Which you also figured out by yourself.

298
00:13:16,440 --> 00:13:19,100
Which I also figured out by myself.

299
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:21,670
Well, I'm glad you're still together.

300
00:13:21,670 --> 00:13:23,110
Yeah, okay, you can stop trying

301
00:13:23,110 --> 00:13:24,860
to make this about our relationship.

302
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:25,890
Which is stronger than ever.

303
00:13:25,890 --> 00:13:28,140
Which is stronger than ever.

304
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:31,240
This is about you trying to change my environment,

305
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:33,150
which was perfect the way it was.

306
00:13:33,150 --> 00:13:36,100
But it got the way it was through change.

307
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:37,960
The-the spot that you love to sit in,

308
00:13:37,970 --> 00:13:39,620
that no one else can sit in,

309
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:41,270
only exists because--

310
00:13:41,270 --> 00:13:44,470
despite your objections-- I bought this couch.

311
00:13:44,470 --> 00:13:45,100
Me.

312
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:47,610
So explain why that change was bad

313
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:49,630
and this change was good.

314
00:13:53,860 --> 00:13:55,400
You don't need to explain yourself to him.

315
00:13:55,400 --> 00:13:58,050
I don't need to explain myself to you!

316
00:13:58,740 --> 00:14:01,130
You're sick of his nonsense and ready to move in with me.

317
00:14:01,130 --> 00:14:03,710
Keep the table! We don't use that space!

318
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:11,560
Damn it, I got cocky.

319
00:14:14,350 --> 00:14:16,710
Hey, Bernie. I'm home... Hello.

320
00:14:16,710 --> 00:14:17,900
Hi, honey.

321
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:19,680
What's going on?

322
00:14:20,120 --> 00:14:22,000
Maybe you should have a seat.

323
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:23,960
Um... okay.

324
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:26,820
I know my mom's not dead; there'd be balloons.

325
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:29,410
First off,

326
00:14:29,410 --> 00:14:32,490
know that we all love you and cherish you.

327
00:14:32,490 --> 00:14:34,770
- Well, I wouldn't... - Daddy.

328
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:36,970
You're great.

329
00:14:38,490 --> 00:14:41,260
- Can I say something? - Oh, of course.

330
00:14:43,010 --> 00:14:44,950
Hey, Fruit Loops.

331
00:14:45,710 --> 00:14:47,450
Massimino? Why are you here?

332
00:14:47,450 --> 00:14:48,700
Well, I heard you were thinking about

333
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:50,990
going back up to the space station,

334
00:14:50,990 --> 00:14:52,920
and as someone who's been there with you...

335
00:14:52,920 --> 00:14:55,730
well, you know how astronauts need to have the "right stuff"?

336
00:14:55,730 --> 00:14:57,090
Sure.

337
00:14:57,090 --> 00:14:59,390
The stuff you have is wrong.

338
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:05,070
You don't think I did a good job up there?

339
00:15:05,070 --> 00:15:06,650
You did a fine job.

340
00:15:06,650 --> 00:15:10,180
It's just, you were scared and miserable the whole time.

341
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:12,330
I think what we're all trying to say is,

342
00:15:12,330 --> 00:15:13,610
you don't seem to be remembering

343
00:15:13,610 --> 00:15:15,970
how traumatic the experience was for you.

344
00:15:15,970 --> 00:15:19,230
L-Like how women often forget the pain of childbirth.

345
00:15:19,230 --> 00:15:22,050
Like a woman. Great analogy.

346
00:15:23,100 --> 00:15:25,350
Fine, maybe I was a little scared.

347
00:15:25,350 --> 00:15:28,310
You peed in your space suit.

348
00:15:28,310 --> 00:15:29,790
You're supposed to do that.

349
00:15:29,790 --> 00:15:32,130
Not during the fitting.

350
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:36,480
Son, do I need to remind you what you asked me to do

351
00:15:36,480 --> 00:15:38,150
before you went up last time?

352
00:15:38,150 --> 00:15:41,050
Hang on. That was just me joking around.

353
00:15:41,050 --> 00:15:44,220
You wanted me to shoot you in the foot.

354
00:15:44,990 --> 00:15:46,340
Come on, how is that not a joke?

355
00:15:46,340 --> 00:15:48,280
"You got to get me out of this. Shoot me in the foot."

356
00:15:50,710 --> 00:15:53,850
Don't forget all the other astronauts picking on you.

357
00:15:53,850 --> 00:15:55,610
And how you threw up in zero gravity,

358
00:15:55,610 --> 00:15:57,900
and it floated back in your mouth.

359
00:15:58,550 --> 00:15:59,770
And you threw up again.

360
00:15:59,770 --> 00:16:02,170
And so on and so on.

361
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,810
That was funny.

362
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:10,480
Okay, so I wasn't exactly John Glenn up there,

363
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:13,530
but I've changed; I'm a different man now.

364
00:16:13,530 --> 00:16:15,270
Yeah, and I'm a little insulted

365
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:17,550
that you guys don't think I can handle it.

366
00:16:17,550 --> 00:16:20,580
You know you're gonna have to go through survival training again.

367
00:16:20,580 --> 00:16:21,530
Really?

368
00:16:21,530 --> 00:16:24,000
You've got to get me out of this!

369
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,460
What do you want me to do? You're perfectly healthy.

370
00:16:26,460 --> 00:16:28,390
Check my blood pressure again!

371
00:16:28,390 --> 00:16:30,810
I can get it higher; just give me a second.

372
00:16:33,070 --> 00:16:35,890
- Hello? - Hi, Ma. How are you?

373
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:38,240
How I am is not dead!

374
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:39,520
But you wouldn't know that,

375
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:42,300
because you don't love me enough to pick up the phone!

376
00:16:42,300 --> 00:16:44,040
Go, go, go!

377
00:16:44,540 --> 00:16:47,700
For all you know, I could have slipped in the tub and drowned!

378
00:16:48,100 --> 00:16:50,970
That's what happened to your Aunt Ida!

379
00:16:58,100 --> 00:17:00,720
Howard, I'm so sorry your blood pressure was off the charts.

380
00:17:00,720 --> 00:17:03,230
Oh, me, too. I mean, the doctor was willing

381
00:17:03,230 --> 00:17:05,030
to fudge the results, but...

382
00:17:05,030 --> 00:17:08,670
it just seemed so darned dishonest.

383
00:17:10,370 --> 00:17:12,420
But the mission wouldn't be for at least a year.

384
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:14,030
Isn't that enough time to get your blood pressure under...

385
00:17:14,030 --> 00:17:17,260
Look, my blood pressure's too high, okay? Drop it.

386
00:17:18,730 --> 00:17:20,420
You know, my aunt changed her diet,

387
00:17:20,420 --> 00:17:22,300
- and in a few months, she... - Went to space?

388
00:17:22,300 --> 00:17:24,390
I don't think so. Now, pass the soy sauce.

389
00:17:24,390 --> 00:17:26,350
Hey, not the green one; the red one.

390
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:30,230
Sheldon, Amy, will you please come join us?

391
00:17:30,270 --> 00:17:31,620
Yeah, it's fun up here.

392
00:17:31,620 --> 00:17:33,960
So why are you trying to ruin it?

393
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:37,280
No, thanks. We're fine.

394
00:17:37,280 --> 00:17:39,650
I mean, if you people want to eat at the table,

395
00:17:39,650 --> 00:17:41,650
then that's what you should do.

396
00:17:41,650 --> 00:17:43,130
I like eating down here

397
00:17:43,130 --> 00:17:46,070
because this is how we've always done things.

398
00:17:46,070 --> 00:17:48,470
But if those days are gone, they're gone.

399
00:17:48,470 --> 00:17:51,420
It just... makes me sad.

400
00:17:52,690 --> 00:17:53,860
Now I feel bad.

401
00:17:53,860 --> 00:17:56,640
Oh, don't anthropomorphize him; he's got big eyes,

402
00:17:56,640 --> 00:17:59,670
but his feelings are not like ours.

403
00:18:01,530 --> 00:18:04,350
No, it just seems silly for us to sit in two groups.

404
00:18:04,350 --> 00:18:06,060
Well, it's not silly if you think of that group

405
00:18:06,060 --> 00:18:09,850
as being led by a big, evil baby.

406
00:18:14,170 --> 00:18:16,390
Look at Amy down there.

407
00:18:16,390 --> 00:18:18,290
Should we go?

408
00:18:18,290 --> 00:18:19,580
Yeah, let's go.

409
00:18:19,580 --> 00:18:21,650
But... Penny, this was your idea.

410
00:18:21,650 --> 00:18:23,550
You said that I should stand up to him.

411
00:18:23,550 --> 00:18:24,640
Forget it, Leonard.

412
00:18:25,040 --> 00:18:26,610
It's over.

413
00:18:27,620 --> 00:18:29,260
Fine.

414
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:36,700
Rajesh?

415
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:38,740
Screw that! I sat on the floor for seven years.

416
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:41,140
I'm staying right here!

417
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:44,810
Come on, Raj, it's not the same without you.

418
00:18:45,210 --> 00:18:46,940
Fine.

419
00:18:48,170 --> 00:18:51,060
But no more making fun of how I say tings.

420
00:18:51,060 --> 00:18:54,070
You mean "tings" like "moo-stache"?

421
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:02,390
Well, isn't this nice.

422
00:19:04,210 --> 00:19:07,100
Sometimes the baby wins.

